Java Does not support Multiple Inheritance. So the class `D` cannot extend 2 classes.

**Correct Code Sample:**

```java
class A {
    public void printt() {
        System.out.println("Class A print method");
    }
}

class B extends A {
    public void printt() {
        System.out.println("Class B print method");
    }
}

class C extends A {
    public void printt() {
        System.out.println("Class C print method");
    }
}

class D extends A {
    public void printt() {
        System.out.println("Class D print method");
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String args[]) {
        D obj = new D();
        obj.printt();
        // access the printt() method of C class using runtime polymorphism
        A aref;
        C cobj = new C();
        aref =cobj;
        aref.printt();
    }
}
